We report the cells of Table~\ref{tab:design} for the unchanged \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer with \texttt{l1\_reg=False} unless another arm is named; the other arms are in the appendix.
The \emph{reference budget} of a cell is the \texttt{shap} default $m=2d+2048$ where that samples ($d\ge16$) and, for $d\le10$, where the default enumerates all coalitions and is exact, the largest sampled budget (half of all coalitions).
At its default budget the explainer enumerates every coalition for $d\le10$, so it is exact there by construction: the evidence at the default budget is German Credit ($d=20$, 40 instances) and ACS Employment ($d=16$, 60), and the three smaller datasets inform the smaller budgets and the other noise sources.

\subsection{The reference is exact and the randomness is where we expect it}
\label{sec:validity}
Exact enumeration and interventional TreeSHAP on the same background agree to a relative $L_2$ distance of at most $4\times10^{-6}$ in every forest and boosted-tree cell except Pima RF (median $8\times10^{-4}$, maximum $0.04$, cause unresolved; the enumeration remains the reference), and for $d\le10$ the explainer at its default budget equals the reference to $5\times10^{-15}$.
For logistic regression the antithetic permutation runs and the \texttt{shap} Kernel runs (\texttt{l1\_reg=False}) have no variance, except at the smallest budgets, where the weighted regression is ill-conditioned and the spread is numerical; the paired Kernel estimator keeps its sampling variance, so the control applies to the other two only.
With $T=R\|\bar{\hat\phi}-\phi\|^2/\operatorname{tr}\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}(\hat\phi)$, about one for an unbiased estimator, the paired Kernel and antithetic permutation estimators and \texttt{l1\_reg=False} are unbiased (median $T=0.9$, $0.9$, $1.0$), while \texttt{num\_features(10)} is not (median $440$) and \texttt{auto} is not for some instances (maximum $>2000$); the biased arms are reported separately.

\subsection{At the default budget one run gets the top-3 set, except near ties}
\label{sec:headline}
Figure~\ref{fig:headline} is the headline.
At the \texttt{shap} default budget of the two $d\ge16$ datasets, the share of instances with $P_c^{\varepsilon}\ge0.9$ is $1.00$ in all eight cells, and the share with strict $P_c\ge0.9$ is $1.00$ for logistic regression and $0.90$--$0.97$ for the other models.
At the reference budget of the $d\le10$ datasets the strict share is $0.79$--$1.00$, the lowest being the random forest on GMSC; the tolerance-aware share is $1.00$ in all $20$ cells.
The tolerance does the work: with $\varepsilon=0$ the share falls by up to $0.21$, with $\varepsilon\ge0.02$ it is $1.00$ everywhere, and the tolerance-aware share is at least $0.98$ at the thresholds $0.8$ and $0.95$ and for $k\in\{1,5\}$ (Table~\ref{tab:sens}).
Below the default the share falls quickly: for the non-linear models of the two larger datasets the tolerance-aware share is $0.97$--$1.00$ at $m=128$, $0.65$--$0.97$ at $m=64$ (three to four times $d$) and $0.00$--$0.02$ at $m=32$ (about twice $d$).
The library's default regularisation is a different setting: \texttt{num\_features(10)} selects ten features by a lasso path and is biased; at the default budget its strict share is $0.68$--$0.93$ on the six non-linear $d\ge16$ cells and $0.78$--$0.87$ for logistic regression, and its tolerance-aware share $0.92$--$1.00$ (Table~\ref{tab:sens}); at $d\le10$ it selects every feature.

One extension reaches $d=279$ (Arrhythmia \citep{guvenir1997arrhythmia}, any arrhythmia against normal; random forest and boosting, $40$ instances, $R=20$, one trained model and one background).
The reference there is interventional TreeSHAP on the same background, not an enumeration; for the forest its attributions miss additivity by more than $10^{-3}$ on $7$ of the $40$ instances.
At the default budget $2d+2048=2606$ the top-3 strict $P_c$, averaged over instances, is $0.99$ (boosting) and $0.95$ (forest) for \texttt{l1\_reg=False}, the tolerance-aware one $1.00$, and the share with strict $P_c\ge0.9$ is $0.975$ and $0.825$; the library default \texttt{num\_features(10)} gives $0.38$ and $0.25$, and at $m=256<d$ \texttt{l1\_reg=False} gives $P_c=0$.
It is one dataset and one split, trees and the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer only: it shows that the unregularised setting holds at the default budget and the library's default regularisation fails at $d=279$, not the budget law or the floors.

