Our pre-specified test of whether aggregate agreement hides them (H1: aggregate agreement is high and the decision-level share at least ten points lower) uses, per cell, the contrast between the mean Pearson correlation and the share with $P_c^{\varepsilon}\ge0.9$ (Table~\ref{tab:h1}).
Read with the tolerance, it is not supported at a default or reference budget: the contrast is within $0.1$ points of zero in every cell, as it must be where $P_c^{\varepsilon}$ forgives near-tie swaps.
Read strictly, with $P_c\ge0.9$ in place of $P_c^{\varepsilon}\ge0.9$, the contrast at the reference budget is $3$ to $21$ points for the non-linear models; it reaches the ten points in the random forests on Pima ($13.0$, interval $7.0$--$19.0$) and GMSC ($21.0$, $13.0$--$29.0$) and its interval excludes zero in $9$ of the $15$ cells.
For the paired Kernel estimator at equal budgets H1 holds in $29$ of the $84$ non-linear cells above the smallest budgets.
For the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer, with the tolerance, at $m=32$ (German Credit, ACS Employment) and $m=13$ (Pima) the contrast is $45$--$90$ points, but the mean Pearson correlation is itself below $0.9$ in $8$ of the $9$ non-linear cells, so the aggregate is not high there and H1 holds only for the Pima MLP.
At budgets of at least $3d$ it holds in $7$ of $93$ non-linear cells: German Credit at $m=64$ for three models ($16$--$33$ points) and one cell each of ACS Employment, ACS Income, GMSC and Pima at $m=25$--$64$.
At every budget of at least $3d$ the mean Pearson correlation of the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer is at least $0.97$.

\subsection{Budget, estimators and the margin law}
\label{sec:budget}
The error of $\hat\phi$ falls with $m$ at a rate that depends on the estimator (Figure~\ref{fig:budget}a).
For the paired Kernel and antithetic permutation estimators the log--log slope of the relative $L_2$ error is $-0.49$ to $-0.53$ on every non-linear cell, as sampling theory says; the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer falls faster, with slopes of $-0.62$ to $-0.66$ on the two larger datasets and $-0.76$ to $-0.91$ on the $d\le10$ ones, while with \texttt{num\_features(10)} the error hardly falls (slope $-0.02$ to $-0.17$) because the estimator is biased.
At equal numbers of model evaluations neither of our estimators beats the unchanged Kernel explainer (Figure~\ref{fig:budget}b): the paired Kernel estimator has a lower share of exact instances by $0.19$--$0.38$ and a lower tolerance-aware share by $0.04$--$0.10$ in all six non-linear cells of the two larger datasets, beyond the smallest resolvable difference, and antithetic permutation is indistinguishable from it (differences $-0.015$ to $+0.001$ in the strict share, none beyond the smallest resolvable difference).
The budget that a margin requires grows as the margin shrinks (Figure~\ref{fig:budget}c,d; Table~\ref{tab:guidance} gives observed budgets per margin quartile, not a formula).
A Gaussian argument suggests $m^*\propto\sigma^2/g^2$ for a pair at margin $g$ and run noise $\sigma/\sqrt m$. We estimate the log--log slope of $m^*$ on the margin by an interval-censored Gaussian regression of $\log m^*$ on $\log g$ (instance-bootstrap interval), because $m^*$ is known only between adjacent grid budgets; it assumes Gaussian residuals, under heavy censoring ($12$--$29$ of $40$--$60$ instances right-censored for the paired Kernel estimator).
For the exact top-3 set on the two $d\ge16$ datasets the slope for the non-linear models is $-1.4$ to $-1.8$ for the paired Kernel estimator ($-2$ inside the interval in two of six cells) and $-1.7$ to $-2.1$ for antithetic permutation sampling ($-2$ inside the interval in all six cells); for the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer it is shallower, $-0.8$ to $-1.2$ on the same models.
The estimate is not stable for the paired Kernel estimator ($-0.65$ to $-1.1$ with every third budget), whereas the permutation slope stays within $-1.6$ to $-2.2$ under every thinning (Appendix~\ref{app:m5}).
The exponent $-2$ is thus supported for antithetic permutation sampling, not refuted for the paired Kernel estimator and unresolved for the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer; a probit fit of the share of correct runs on $g\sqrt m/\sigma$, with $\sigma$ estimated per instance from the other budgets, gives for permutation sampling a coefficient of $0.93$--$1.05$ at $m_{\mathrm{eff}}\ge256$ against the predicted $1$.
For the tolerance-aware criterion, $m^*$ depends little on the margin (slope $+0.1$ to $-0.5$), and on the $d\le10$ datasets the slope is uninformative ($59$--$98\%$ right-censored for the paired Kernel estimator).

