\subsection{A one-run certificate against running twice: a mixed result}
\label{sec:cert}
A user who wants to know whether a run is reliable can either attach a certificate to one paired Kernel run (the probability, under the run's own estimated covariance, that the top-3 set is unchanged) or run twice and read the overlap.
We compare their calibrated probabilities of top-3 correctness by the Brier score, paired over instances, with the pre-specified useful margin of $0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:cert} in Appendix~\ref{app:tables}).
Both are approximately calibrated on held-out instances, and the variance estimate behind the certificate has coverage $0.90$--$0.95$ of the exact attributions against a nominal $0.95$.
The pre-specified contrast, in which each method predicts the correctness of the run it delivers, favours running twice on all five datasets ($+0.019$ on German Credit to $+0.049$ on Pima in Brier score, positive meaning the certificate is worse).
The two methods are scored on different delivered runs with different base rates, the certificate delivering the longer run (base rate of correct runs $0.31$--$0.53$ against $0.19$--$0.41$; Table~\ref{tab:cert}a); when the run-twice user instead delivers the mean of the two runs, which matches the certificate's deliverable, the certificate is better on all five ($-0.014$ to $-0.025$).
As a stopping rule at a $0.9$ threshold the certificate reaches the same share of exact instances with $20.4\%$ ($95\%$ interval $16.6$--$24.5$) fewer evaluations than a flat budget on German Credit and $22.6\%$ ($18.1$--$27.6$) on ACS Employment, and with $1.7\%$ and $1.4\%$ on ACS Income and GMSC (not defined on Pima), while stopping on agreement of two runs leaves a lower share exact and costs more than a flat run (Table~\ref{tab:cert}b).
The certificate is thus better only when the competitor delivers the same quantity.

